    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d657">

                <pb facs="0737" n="423" xml:id="pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 611.419/34</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate General</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-11-06">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">November 6, 1914</hi>.</date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 4 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>My October 29.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d657-f1"/> To my request that American
                    importers be permitted to obtain delivery of all wool contracted for prior to
                    October 6, Board Trade replies to-day no exceptions can at present be made in
                    favor of such contracts. Permits will be granted only when shown that such wool
                    is for manufacture goods for Allied governments.</p>
                <p>Inform American Woolen Company.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Skinner</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
